@page "/cookies"
@page "/cookie-policy"

<PageTitle>Cookie Policy | Comfidiario</PageTitle>
<SeoMetadata Title="Cookie Policy | Comfidiario"
             Description="Understand the cookies and browser storage used by Comfidiario."
             CanonicalPath="/cookies"
             Robots="index, follow" />

<main class="legal-page">
    <article class="legal-document">
        <p class="legal-eyebrow">Legal &amp; Privacy</p>
        <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">Based on the current application configuration; deployment settings may affect cookie behavior.</p>

        <section>
            <h2>What the application uses</h2>
            <p>
                The application uses cookies and browser storage for core functionality. It does not intentionally
                use advertising cookies, analytics cookies, or session replay technology. Hosting and identity
                providers may set or record their own security information under their own policies.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Authentication cookie</h2>
            <p>
                The application issues an HTTP-only authentication cookie to keep a signed-in session. Its
                configured lifetime is up to seven days. The production configuration requires the cookie
                to be sent over HTTPS and uses SameSite=Lax. The browser does not expose this HTTP-only
                cookie to ordinary page scripts.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Session cookie</h2>
            <p>
                The application configures a session cookie and a 30-minute session idle timeout. Its
                Secure setting depends on the environment: HTTPS is required in production, while local
                development follows the request scheme.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Antiforgery tokens</h2>
            <p>
                Antiforgery tokens help protect state-changing forms, such as sign-in, sign-out, and account
                deletion, from cross-site request forgery. They are security functionality, not analytics.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Optional Google sign-in</h2>
            <p>
                If Google sign-in is configured, the application uses a separate short-lived external
                authentication cookie during the sign-in flow. Google may also process information under
                its own policies.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Local storage and third-party resources</h2>
            <p>
                Anonymous journal content is stored in the browser's local storage under an application
                storage key. This is browser storage, not a cookie. The application does not load Google Fonts,
                jsDelivr scripts, or advertising and analytics scripts; Google sign-in is the only optional
                third-party identity provider and is active only when configured.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Managing browser storage</h2>
            <p>
                You can clear cookies and local storage through your browser settings. Clearing the
                application's local storage removes anonymous journal data saved on that browser. Clearing
                authentication cookies signs you out. Some features may not work as intended if required
                security cookies or storage are blocked.
            </p>
        </section>

        <p class="legal-note">
            This page describes the application's source configuration. The operator should verify the
            deployed configuration and any provider-set cookies before launch.
        </p>

        <nav class="legal-page-links" aria-label="Other legal pages">
            <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>
            <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>
            <a href="/contact">Contact &amp; Support</a>
        </nav>
    </article>
</main>
